      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sporadic gaps, even when everything *looks* perfect on paper, can be the most maddening kind of defect. It's less about a grand, sweeping failure and more about tiny, localized failures, which often points to inconsistent application or minor environmental fluctuations. Think about a tiny, very fast-moving, highly localized problem, like a microscopic dust particle or a fleeting temperature fluctuation in a specific zone of the press. It's almost like a tiny, invisible streak of bad luck hitting the print head, which is a very common, albeit frustrating, occurrence.",
